// Unit 3: Options. Chapter 1: Options From Zero.
export default {
  id: 'options-ch1',
  title: 'Options From Zero',
  blurb: 'Calls, puts, strikes and premiums: what an option really is and how one is born, lives and expires.',
  lessons: [
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
    {
      id: 'options-1-what-is-an-option',
      title: 'A Right, Not a Promise',
      summary: `An option is a contract that gives you a choice. Learn what that choice is, who sits on each side, and why these contracts exist at all.`,
      minutes: 13,
      icon: 'lightbulb',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The concert deposit', html: `<p>Maya's favorite band announces a tour. Tickets are <strong>$150</strong> today, but she gets paid in three weeks. The venue makes an offer: pay a <strong>$20 non-refundable deposit</strong> now, and you can buy a ticket at $150 any time in the next 30 days.</p><p>Two weeks later the show is selling out and resale tickets hit <span class="up">$250</span>. Maya pays her $150 and walks in. Her $20 bought her the right to a $250 ticket for $150.</p><p>Now flip it. Say the band cancels half the shows, and tickets drop to $90 on the resale site. Maya simply does <em>not</em> use her deposit. She loses the <span class="down">$20</span>, and that is the end of it. No one forces her to pay $150 for a $90 ticket.</p><p>That deposit is an option, minus the paperwork: a small payment today for the <span class="hl">right, but not the obligation</span>, to buy something at a locked price before a deadline.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: `Pay a little now to keep a choice open later. That is the whole idea. The rest of this unit is details. Important details.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'What an option actually is', html: `<p>A financial <span class="term" data-def="A contract giving the buyer the right, but not the obligation, to buy or sell an asset at a set price on or before a set date.">option</span> is a contract with four ingredients:</p><ul><li><strong>The underlying:</strong> the thing the contract is about, usually 100 shares of a stock or ETF, or a stock index.</li><li><strong>The strike price:</strong> the locked-in price at which you can buy or sell. Maya's strike was $150.</li><li><strong>The expiration date:</strong> the deadline. After it, the contract is gone.</li><li><strong>The premium:</strong> the price the buyer pays for the contract. Maya's premium was $20.</li></ul><p>The buyer pays the premium up front and gets the choice. If using the right is worth it, they use it (or sell the contract to someone else). If not, they let it expire. The most a buyer can lose is the premium they paid.</p><p>One key difference from Maya's deposit: in her deal the $20 did not count toward the ticket. Same with options. The premium is a separate cost, gone either way, which is why it matters so much in every calculation you will do.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'The four ingredients', cards: [
          { front: 'Underlying', back: `The asset the option is about, such as 100 shares of a stock, an ETF, or an index.` },
          { front: 'Strike price', back: `The fixed price at which the option lets you buy or sell the underlying.` },
          { front: 'Expiration', back: `The last day the option exists. After that it is either used or worthless.` },
          { front: 'Premium', back: `What the buyer pays the seller for the option. It is not refunded.` },
          { front: 'Holder', back: `Another name for the buyer, the person who owns the right.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'check', question: `Maya paid a $20 deposit to lock a $150 ticket price. If ticket prices crash to $60, what is the most she can lose on the deposit deal?`, options: [`$20, the deposit`, `$90, the drop from $150 to $60`, `$150, the full ticket price`, `Nothing, since she did not buy`], answer: 0, explain: `She can walk away. The right expires unused, and her loss is capped at the $20 she paid for it.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Two sides of every contract', html: `<p>Every option has a buyer and a seller. The seller is also called the <span class="term" data-def="The person who sells an option to open a position, collecting the premium and taking on an obligation.">writer</span>, because they create (write) the contract.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Buyer (holder)</th><th>Seller (writer)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Premium</td><td>Pays it</td><td>Collects it</td></tr><tr><td>Has a...</td><td>Right</td><td>Obligation</td></tr><tr><td>Best case</td><td>Can be large</td><td>Keep the premium</td></tr><tr><td>Worst case</td><td>Lose the premium</td><td>Can be large, sometimes unlimited</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The seller cannot walk away. If the buyer decides to use the right, the seller must deliver. That obligation is what the premium pays for.</p><p>Before fees, options are a <span class="hl">zero-sum contract</span>: every dollar one side makes on the contract, the other side loses. Someone is on the other end of every trade you place.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Buyers risk a known amount. Sellers collect a small known amount and can lose much more. Always know which side you are on.` } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `If a trade goes badly for the seller of an option, the seller can choose not to honor the contract and simply lose the premium.`, answer: false, explain: `Only the buyer has a choice. The seller took the premium in exchange for an obligation and must deliver if the buyer exercises.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Insurance: the option you already know', html: `<p>Leo pays $90 a month for car insurance. Most months, nothing happens and the insurer keeps his money. The month he gets rear-ended, the insurer pays for a $4,000 repair.</p><p>That is an option in disguise. Leo is the <strong>buyer</strong>: he pays a premium for the right to be made whole if something bad happens. The insurance company is the <strong>seller</strong>: it collects lots of small premiums and occasionally pays a big claim.</p><p>Stock investors can buy the same kind of protection. A contract called a <strong>put</strong> can lock in a price at which you are allowed to sell your shares, no matter how far they fall. You will build one later in this unit.</p><p>Notice the word <em>premium</em> is the same in both worlds. That is not an accident. Options and insurance run on the same math: price the chance of something happening, then charge for it.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Calls and puts in one breath', html: `<p>There are only two kinds of options, and every strategy in this unit is built from them.</p><ul><li>A <span class="term" data-def="An option giving the buyer the right to buy the underlying at the strike price.">call</span> gives the buyer the right to <strong>buy</strong> the underlying at the strike price.</li><li>A <span class="term" data-def="An option giving the buyer the right to sell the underlying at the strike price.">put</span> gives the buyer the right to <strong>sell</strong> the underlying at the strike price.</li></ul><p>Memory trick: you <span class="hl">call</span> something up to you (buy it), and you <span class="hl">put</span> something away from you (sell it). Call buyers usually want the price to go up. Put buyers usually want it to go down, or want protection in case it does.</p><p>Each kind can be bought or sold, so there are four basic positions: long call, short call, long put, short put. "Long" means you bought it. "Short" means you sold it to open.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `Match each position to what it gives or requires.`, pairs: [
          { left: 'Call buyer', right: 'Right to buy at the strike' },
          { left: 'Put buyer', right: 'Right to sell at the strike' },
          { left: 'Call seller', right: 'Must sell at the strike if assigned' },
          { left: 'Put seller', right: 'Must buy at the strike if assigned' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why options exist', html: `<p>Options trade on exchanges with standardized terms, and every listed US option is cleared by the <span class="term" data-def="The Options Clearing Corporation, the central clearinghouse that stands between buyers and sellers of US listed options.">OCC</span>, which stands in the middle so you never have to trust a stranger to pay. The first US options exchange, the Chicago Board Options Exchange, opened in 1973.</p><p>People use them for four big reasons:</p><ul><li><strong>Hedging:</strong> protecting something you own, like Leo's insurance.</li><li><strong>Income:</strong> sellers collect premiums, the way an insurer does.</li><li><strong>Speculation:</strong> a small premium can control a big chunk of stock, so gains (and losses) are amplified.</li><li><strong>Betting on movement itself:</strong> some strategies profit from a big move in either direction, or from no move at all.</li></ul>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Options are ancient. Aristotle told a story about Thales locking in olive presses before a big harvest. The exchange version is newer.` } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'Leverage cuts both ways', html: `<p>A $200 option can move like $5,000 worth of stock. That sounds amazing until you realize the option can also go to <span class="down">$0</span> while the stock barely moves. Options have deadlines. Stocks do not. Plenty of option buyers are right about the direction and still lose money because the move came too late or was too small.</p>` },
        { type: 'order', prompt: `Put the life of a typical option trade in order.`, items: [`Buyer pays the premium to the seller`, `The underlying price moves over time`, `Buyer chooses to sell, exercise, or hold`, `The contract reaches expiration and ends`], explain: `Premium changes hands first. Then the market moves, the buyer decides what to do, and expiration ends the contract no matter what.` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `What does the buyer of an option get?`, options: [`An obligation to buy or sell`, `A right, but not an obligation, to buy or sell`, `Ownership of the underlying shares`, `A guaranteed profit if held to expiration`], answer: 1, explain: `The buyer pays a premium for a choice. They never have to use it.` },
          { q: `Who collects the premium?`, options: [`The exchange`, `The buyer`, `The seller (writer)`, `The OCC`], answer: 2, explain: `The buyer pays the premium to the seller, who takes on the obligation in return.` },
          { q: `A put option gives its buyer the right to...`, options: [`Buy the underlying at the strike`, `Receive dividends`, `Sell the underlying at the strike`, `Extend the expiration date`], answer: 2, explain: `Puts are the right to sell. Calls are the right to buy.` },
          { q: `Leo's car insurance is most like which position?`, options: [`Owning a put, a paid-for right to be protected`, `Selling a call`, `Selling a put`, `Owning a stock`], answer: 0, explain: `Leo pays a premium for protection against a loss, which is exactly what a put buyer does for a stock.` },
          { q: `Which statement about the maximum loss for an option buyer is true?`, options: [`It is unlimited`, `It equals the strike price`, `It depends on the seller`, `It is the premium paid`], answer: 3, explain: `If the option expires worthless, the buyer loses what they paid and nothing more.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
    {
      id: 'options-1-call-options',
      title: 'Calls: The Right to Buy',
      summary: `How a call option makes or loses money, how to find its breakeven, and why the buyer can never lose more than the premium.`,
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'bull',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Meet LMNB', html: `<p>Throughout this unit we will trade a made-up stock called <strong>LMNB</strong> (Lemon Bay Beverages). It trades at <strong>$50</strong> a share.</p><p>Leo thinks LMNB is about to pop after a new product launch. He could buy 100 shares for $5,000. Instead, he looks at a <strong>call option</strong>:</p><ul><li>Strike: <strong>$55</strong></li><li>Expiration: about <strong>30 days</strong> away</li><li>Premium: <strong>$2.00 per share</strong></li></ul><p>One standard US stock option covers <span class="hl">100 shares</span>, and premiums are quoted per share. So this contract costs $2.00 x 100 = <strong>$200</strong>, plus any small broker fees.</p><p>For $200, Leo gets the right to buy 100 LMNB shares at $55 any time before expiration. If LMNB is trading at $65 by then, that right is very valuable. If LMNB is sitting at $51, nobody would pay $55 for it, and the right is worthless.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: `$200 instead of $5,000 to bet on the same stock? That is the leverage everyone talks about. Hold that thought.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'What the call is worth at expiration', html: `<p>On expiration day, a call is worth exactly how far the stock is <em>above</em> the strike, and never less than zero:</p><p><span class="hl">Call value at expiration = the larger of (stock price - strike) or 0</span></p><p>Then subtract what you paid to get your profit or loss. Here is Leo's 55 call bought for $2.00:</p><table><thead><tr><th>LMNB at expiration</th><th>Call worth</th><th>Leo's P/L (x100)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$45</td><td>$0</td><td><span class="down">-$200</span></td></tr><tr><td>$55</td><td>$0</td><td><span class="down">-$200</span></td></tr><tr><td>$56</td><td>$1</td><td><span class="down">-$100</span></td></tr><tr><td>$57</td><td>$2</td><td>$0</td></tr><tr><td>$60</td><td>$5</td><td><span class="up">+$300</span></td></tr><tr><td>$65</td><td>$10</td><td><span class="up">+$800</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Notice that no matter how far LMNB falls, Leo loses the same $200. Below the strike, the call is simply worthless.</p>` },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'call-payoff', caption: `The long call payoff is flat at the premium loss below the strike, then rises one-for-one with the stock above it. It crosses zero at the breakeven.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Breakeven: where the money starts', html: `<p>Above $55 the call has value, but Leo already spent $2.00 per share. He needs the stock to climb far enough to pay that back first.</p><p><span class="hl">Call breakeven at expiration = strike + premium</span></p><p>For Leo: $55 + $2.00 = <strong>$57</strong>. LMNB has to rise from $50 to $57, a 14% gain, before Leo makes a single dollar at expiration. Between $55 and $57 the option has some value, so he loses less than $200, but he still loses.</p><p>This is the first big lesson of buying options: <strong>you do not just need to be right about direction. You need to be right by enough.</strong> A stock can go up 10% and the call buyer can still lose money.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A call has a $40 strike and costs $3.00. What is its breakeven stock price at expiration?`, answer: 43, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: `Strike plus premium: $40 + $3.00 = $43. Below that, the buyer has a loss at expiration.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Leverage, both directions', html: `<p>Compare Leo's two choices if LMNB rises to $62 by expiration:</p><ul><li><strong>Buy 100 shares for $5,000:</strong> profit $12 x 100 = $1,200, a <span class="up">+24%</span> return.</li><li><strong>Buy the 55 call for $200:</strong> worth $7 x 100 = $700, profit $500, a <span class="up">+250%</span> return.</li></ul><p>Now say LMNB rises to $54. Still a nice move.</p><ul><li><strong>Shares:</strong> profit $400, <span class="up">+8%</span>.</li><li><strong>Call:</strong> worthless. <span class="down">-100%</span>. All $200 gone.</li></ul><p>Same stock, same direction, opposite outcome. That is what leverage means: <span class="hl">small moves in the stock cause huge percentage moves in the option</span>, both ways.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `The call buyer's loss is capped at $200 in dollars, but it is 100% of what they put in. That happens a lot.` } },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'option-payoff', props: { kind: 'call', side: 'long', strike: 55, premium: 2, spot: 50 }, caption: `Drag the stock price and watch Leo's 55 call. Find the breakeven, then try changing the premium and see how it shifts.` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Leo bought one 55 call for $2.00. LMNB closes at $60 on expiration day. What is his total profit in dollars?`, answer: 300, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `The call is worth $60 - $55 = $5 per share. Subtract the $2 premium for $3 per share, times 100 shares = $300.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The other side: selling a call', html: `<p>Someone sold Leo that call and pocketed his $200. The seller's results are Leo's results with the sign flipped.</p><ul><li>If LMNB ends at or below $55, the seller keeps the full <span class="up">$200</span>. That is their maximum profit.</li><li>If LMNB ends at $65, the seller must deliver shares worth $65 for $55, a $10 loss per share, offset by the $2 premium: <span class="down">-$800</span>.</li></ul><p>If the seller does not own the shares (a <span class="term" data-def="Selling a call without owning the underlying shares. The potential loss has no ceiling.">naked call</span>), there is no ceiling on how high a stock can go, so the potential loss is <strong>theoretically unlimited</strong>. Sellers who own the shares (a covered call) have a very different risk, which you will learn in the strategies chapter.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why some calls cost more than others', html: `<p>Leo's 55 call cost $2.00. Other LMNB calls with the same expiration cost very different amounts. Three things push a call's price up:</p><ul><li><strong>A lower strike.</strong> A 50 call is already closer to paying off than a 55 call, so it costs more. A 60 call costs less.</li><li><strong>More time.</strong> A call with 90 days left gives the stock three times as long to move as a 30-day call, so it costs more.</li><li><strong>A jumpier stock.</strong> If LMNB regularly swings 5% in a day, big moves above the strike are more likely, and sellers charge for that.</li></ul><p>None of these make a call a better or worse deal by themselves. The price is the market's estimate of the odds. A pricier call is more likely to pay off. A cheap call is cheap for a reason. Chapter 2 is all about how these forces combine.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'myth', title: 'Myth: cheap calls are a bargain', html: `<p>A call quoted at $0.05 costs just $5. It feels like a free lottery ticket. But the market priced it at a nickel because it rarely pays off. Buying lots of very cheap, far-away calls is a common way beginners lose money one small bet at a time. Low price is not the same as good value.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'You do not have to wait for expiration', html: `<p>The payoff table shows value <em>on expiration day</em>. Before that, a call usually trades for more than its expiration value because there is still time left for the stock to move. Most option buyers sell their contracts before expiration instead of holding to the end. You will learn why in the lessons on moneyness and exercise.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: `Dev buys a 30 call for $1.50. The stock rallies from $28 to $31 by expiration. What happened?`, options: [`He lost $50 on the contract`, `He made $150 because the stock went up`, `He made $100, the amount above the strike`, `He lost the full $150`], answer: 0, explain: `The call is worth $1 per share at expiration. He paid $1.50, so he lost $0.50 x 100 = $50 even though the stock rose.` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Call cheat sheet', cards: [
          { front: 'Long call max loss', back: `The premium paid, times 100 per contract.` },
          { front: 'Long call max gain', back: `Unlimited in theory, since a stock has no price ceiling.` },
          { front: 'Call breakeven', back: `Strike plus premium, measured at expiration.` },
          { front: 'Short call max gain', back: `The premium collected.` },
          { front: 'Naked short call max loss', back: `Theoretically unlimited.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `A 70 call costs $4.00. What is the breakeven at expiration?`, options: [`$66`, `$70`, `$74`, `$78`], answer: 2, explain: `Strike plus premium: $70 + $4 = $74.` },
          { q: `How much does one contract of a call quoted at $1.25 cost, before fees?`, options: [`$1.25`, `$12.50`, `$125`, `$1,250`], answer: 2, explain: `Premiums are per share and one contract covers 100 shares: $1.25 x 100 = $125.` },
          { q: `Leo buys a 20 call for $0.80. The stock ends at $18. His result?`, options: [`He loses $80`, `He loses $200`, `He loses $280`, `He breaks even`], answer: 0, explain: `The call expires worthless, so he loses the $0.80 premium x 100 = $80. His loss cannot exceed what he paid.` },
          { q: `Which position has theoretically unlimited loss?`, options: [`Long call`, `Naked short call`, `Long put`, `Owning 100 shares`], answer: 1, explain: `A naked call seller must deliver shares at the strike no matter how high the stock goes.` },
          { q: `A stock goes from $50 to $53. A 55 call bought for $1.00 is held to expiration. Return on the option?`, options: [`+6%`, `+200%`, `0%`, `-100%`], answer: 3, explain: `At $53 the 55 call is worthless at expiration. The stock gained 6%, but the option lost everything.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
    {
      id: 'options-1-put-options',
      title: 'Puts: The Right to Sell',
      summary: `Puts gain value when prices fall. See how to calculate their payoff and breakeven, and how investors use them as insurance.`,
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'bear',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Flipping the call', html: `<p>A <strong>put</strong> is the mirror image of a call. It gives the buyer the right to <strong>sell</strong> 100 shares at the strike price before expiration.</p><p>Why would that be valuable? Imagine owning a coupon that says "Someone must buy your LMNB shares for $45." If LMNB crashes to $30, that coupon lets you dump $30 shares for $45. The further the stock falls, the more the coupon is worth.</p><p>Dev thinks LMNB, now at <strong>$50</strong>, is overhyped. He buys one put:</p><ul><li>Strike: <strong>$45</strong></li><li>Expiration: about <strong>30 days</strong></li><li>Premium: <strong>$1.50</strong> per share, so <strong>$150</strong> for the contract</li></ul><p>If LMNB stays above $45, the put expires worthless and Dev loses $150. If it falls well below $45, the put pays off.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: `Calls love rallies. Puts love drops. Same machinery, pointed the other way.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Put value at expiration', html: `<p>At expiration, a put is worth how far the stock is <em>below</em> the strike, never less than zero:</p><p><span class="hl">Put value at expiration = the larger of (strike - stock price) or 0</span></p><table><thead><tr><th>LMNB at expiration</th><th>Put worth</th><th>Dev's P/L (x100)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$55</td><td>$0</td><td><span class="down">-$150</span></td></tr><tr><td>$45</td><td>$0</td><td><span class="down">-$150</span></td></tr><tr><td>$44</td><td>$1</td><td><span class="down">-$50</span></td></tr><tr><td>$43.50</td><td>$1.50</td><td>$0</td></tr><tr><td>$40</td><td>$5</td><td><span class="up">+$350</span></td></tr><tr><td>$30</td><td>$15</td><td><span class="up">+$1,350</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p><span class="hl">Put breakeven at expiration = strike - premium</span>. For Dev: $45 - $1.50 = <strong>$43.50</strong>.</p>` },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'put-payoff', caption: `The long put loses only the premium when the stock is above the strike, then gains as the stock falls. Its profit is capped because a stock cannot go below zero.` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `An 80 put costs $4.00. What is its breakeven stock price at expiration?`, answer: 76, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: `For puts, subtract: $80 - $4 = $76. The stock must fall below $76 for the buyer to profit at expiration.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'How much can a put make?', html: `<p>A call's upside is unlimited because a stock has no ceiling. A put's upside has a floor, because a stock cannot fall below <strong>$0</strong>.</p><p><span class="hl">Long put max profit = (strike - premium) x 100</span></p><p>For Dev's 45 put: ($45 - $1.50) x 100 = <strong>$4,350</strong>, which only happens if LMNB goes to zero. His max loss stays at the $150 premium.</p><p>Compare that with <span class="term" data-def="Borrowing shares and selling them, hoping to buy them back cheaper later. Losses grow without limit if the price rises.">short selling</span>, the other common way to bet on a drop. A short seller borrows shares and sells them. If the stock rockets instead, the short seller's loss has no ceiling. The put buyer's worst case is fixed from day one. That defined risk is a big reason people bet on declines with puts instead.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Fun asymmetry: a long call can make unlimited money, a long put cannot, because prices stop at zero but not at the top.` } },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'option-payoff', props: { kind: 'put', side: 'long', strike: 45, premium: 1.5, spot: 50 }, caption: `Move the stock price and watch Dev's 45 put. Check that the P/L line crosses zero at $43.50.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Puts as insurance', html: `<p>Here is where puts get really useful. Leo already owns <strong>100 shares of LMNB</strong> at $50. Earnings are coming and he is nervous, but he does not want to sell.</p><p>He buys the same 45 put for $150. Now his worst case is locked in. If LMNB crashes to $30:</p><ul><li>His shares lose $20 x 100 = <span class="down">-$2,000</span></li><li>His put is worth $15 x 100 = <span class="up">+$1,500</span></li><li>Minus the $150 premium</li></ul><p>Net: <strong>-$650</strong>, no matter how far the stock falls. That is $5 per share of drop down to the strike, plus the $1.50 premium. It works just like car insurance: the gap between $50 and the $45 strike is his <span class="term" data-def="The part of a loss you pay yourself before insurance kicks in.">deductible</span>, and the $150 is his premium.</p><p>If LMNB rallies instead, the put expires worthless and Leo's shares enjoy the ride, minus $150. Insurance you did not use is not wasted. It is just what certainty costs.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Choosing your deductible', html: `<p>Leo could pick a different strike for his insurance. Closer protection costs more, just like a low-deductible car policy. Here are three LMNB puts with the same expiration, stock at $50:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Put strike</th><th>Premium</th><th>Cost (x100)</th><th>Worst case for Leo</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$50</td><td>$3.00</td><td>$300</td><td><span class="down">-$300</span></td></tr><tr><td>$45</td><td>$1.50</td><td>$150</td><td><span class="down">-$650</span></td></tr><tr><td>$40</td><td>$0.60</td><td>$60</td><td><span class="down">-$1,060</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The worst case is the drop from $50 to the strike, plus the premium, times 100. The 50 put protects from the first penny but costs the most. The 40 put is cheap, but Leo eats the first $10 per share of losses himself.</p><p>There is no correct answer. It depends on how much loss Leo can stomach and how much he is willing to pay for calm.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'Insurance gets pricier when people are scared', html: `<p>When markets fall hard, lots of investors rush to buy puts at once, and put prices jump, the same way storm insurance gets expensive right after a hurricane. The <strong>VIX</strong>, often called the market's fear gauge, is calculated from the prices of S&amp;P 500 index options. When it spikes, protection costs more. The cheapest time to buy insurance is usually when nobody feels like they need it.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Ms. Ortiz buys a 30 put for $2.00. The stock is at $25 at expiration. What is her total profit in dollars on one contract?`, answer: 300, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `The put is worth $30 - $25 = $5 per share. Subtract the $2 premium for $3, times 100 = $300.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Selling a put', html: `<p>The put seller collects the premium and takes on an obligation: <strong>buy 100 shares at the strike</strong> if assigned.</p><ul><li>If LMNB stays above $45, the seller keeps the <span class="up">$150</span>.</li><li>If LMNB falls to $35, the seller must pay $45 for shares worth $35: a $10 per share hit, minus the $1.50 collected, for <span class="down">-$850</span>.</li></ul><p><span class="hl">Short put max loss = (strike - premium) x 100</span>, which is $4,350 here if the stock goes to zero. That is large but not unlimited.</p><p>Some investors sell puts on stocks they would be happy to own, treating the strike as their buy price and getting paid to wait. That strategy has a name, the cash-secured put, and gets its own lesson later.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Selling puts feels like free money until the stock gaps down. Then you are buying it at a price that now looks silly.` } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Same rule as calls: you can sell early', html: `<p>The tables above show results on expiration day. Before then, a put trades for its intrinsic value plus some extra for the time remaining. If LMNB drops to $44 two weeks early, Dev's put might be worth more than $1 because there is still time for further drops. Most put buyers who profit simply sell the put back before expiration rather than exercising it.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `Buying a put can lose more than the premium you paid if the stock rises a lot.`, answer: false, explain: `A long put's worst case is expiring worthless. You lose the premium, nothing more, however high the stock goes.` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `Match each position to its maximum loss.`, pairs: [
          { left: 'Long put', right: 'The premium paid' },
          { left: 'Short put', right: 'Strike minus premium, times 100' },
          { left: 'Long call', right: 'The premium paid, too' },
          { left: 'Naked short call', right: 'No ceiling' },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `A put option gains value mainly when...`, options: [`The stock rises`, `The stock falls`, `Dividends increase`, `The strike rises after purchase`], answer: 1, explain: `A put is the right to sell at the strike, which becomes more valuable as the market price drops below it.` },
          { q: `A 60 put costs $2.50. Breakeven at expiration?`, options: [`$62.50`, `$60.00`, `$57.50`, `$55.00`], answer: 2, explain: `Strike minus premium: $60 - $2.50 = $57.50.` },
          { q: `What is the maximum profit on a 20 put bought for $1.00?`, options: [`Unlimited`, `$100`, `$2,000`, `$1,900`], answer: 3, explain: `If the stock goes to zero, the put is worth $20. Minus the $1 premium = $19 x 100 = $1,900.` },
          { q: `Leo owns 100 shares at $50 and buys a 45 put for $1.50. What is his worst-case loss at expiration?`, options: [`$650`, `$150`, `$500`, `$5,000`], answer: 0, explain: `He loses $5 per share down to the strike plus $1.50 premium, so $6.50 x 100 = $650.` },
          { q: `Why might someone buy a put instead of short selling a stock?`, options: [`Puts never lose money`, `Puts pay dividends`, `The put's maximum loss is fixed at the premium`, `Short selling is illegal`], answer: 2, explain: `A short seller faces unlimited loss if the stock soars. A put buyer can only lose the premium.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
    {
      id: 'options-1-contract-mechanics',
      title: 'Reading the Fine Print',
      summary: `The 100-share multiplier, strikes, expiration cycles, option symbols and how to read an option chain without getting fooled by the spread.`,
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'receipt',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The x100 rule', html: `<p>Listed options are <span class="term" data-def="Made with identical, exchange-set terms so any two contracts with the same details are interchangeable.">standardized</span>. Every standard US stock or ETF option covers <strong>100 shares</strong>, and every price you see is <strong>per share</strong>.</p><p>So when an app shows an LMNB call at <strong>$2.50</strong>, buying one contract actually costs:</p><p><span class="hl">$2.50 x 100 = $250</span></p><p>This trips up beginners constantly. Maya, practicing in a paper-trading simulator since she is only 17, once saw a call "for $0.40" and thought she was spending forty cents. She was spending $40. Buying 10 of them is $400.</p><p>On top of the premium, many brokers charge a small fee per contract, often well under a dollar, and tiny regulatory fees apply too. Some brokers advertise $0 option commissions. Either way, check your broker's fee page, because fees add up fast if you trade a lot of contracts.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'wow', say: `Read every option price, then say "times one hundred" out loud. Your wallet will thank you.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Leo buys 3 call contracts quoted at $1.20. How much premium does he pay in total, before fees?`, answer: 360, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `$1.20 per share x 100 shares x 3 contracts = $360.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Strikes and expiration dates', html: `<p><strong>Strikes</strong> come in set steps. A popular stock might list strikes every $1, $2.50 or $5, with more choices near the current price and for heavily traded names.</p><p><strong>Expirations</strong> come in a few flavors:</p><ul><li><strong>Monthlies:</strong> the classic cycle, expiring on the <span class="hl">third Friday</span> of the month.</li><li><strong>Weeklies:</strong> expire on other Fridays, giving short-dated choices every week for many popular stocks and ETFs.</li><li><strong>Daily expirations:</strong> a few very heavily traded products, like the S&amp;P 500 index (SPX) and big ETFs such as SPY and QQQ, have expirations every trading day.</li><li><strong>LEAPS:</strong> long-term options that expire more than a year out.</li></ul><p>If an expiration Friday is a market holiday, the options usually expire the trading day before. And on expiration day, standard stock options stop trading at the 4:00 p.m. ET close.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Decoding an option symbol', html: `<p>Your app might show a contract as <strong>LMNB Nov 20 2026 55 Call</strong>. Behind the scenes, US options use a standard code that packs the same facts into one string:</p><p><span class="hl">LMNB 261120 C 00055000</span></p><ul><li><strong>LMNB</strong>: the underlying</li><li><strong>261120</strong>: expiration date, year-month-day (2026, November, 20th)</li><li><strong>C</strong>: call (a put shows P)</li><li><strong>00055000</strong>: the strike times 1,000, so $55.000</li></ul><p>You rarely need to type this yourself, but it is printed on trade confirmations and tax forms. If you can read it, you can always confirm you traded exactly what you meant to: right stock, right date, right strike, right type. Clicking a put when you meant a call is a real and painful mistake.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'happy', say: `November 20, 2026 is the third Friday of the month, so that contract is a standard monthly. Calendars are secretly fun.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The option chain', html: `<p>An <span class="term" data-def="A table listing all available option contracts for one underlying and expiration, with prices and activity.">option chain</span> lists every contract for one expiration. Calls usually sit on the left, puts on the right, strikes down the middle. Here is a slice for LMNB at $50:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Call bid</th><th>Call ask</th><th>Strike</th><th>Put bid</th><th>Put ask</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5.70</td><td>5.90</td><td>45</td><td>0.65</td><td>0.75</td></tr><tr><td>2.40</td><td>2.60</td><td>50</td><td>2.25</td><td>2.45</td></tr><tr><td>0.75</td><td>0.85</td><td>55</td><td>5.55</td><td>5.80</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Real chains add more columns: <strong>last</strong> (the most recent trade), <strong>volume</strong> (contracts traded today) and <strong>open interest</strong> (contracts still open). Many apps also show the Greeks, which you will meet in Chapter 2.</p>` },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'option-chain', props: { spot: 50 }, caption: `Tap different strikes on this illustrative chain. Notice how call prices fall as strikes rise, while put prices do the opposite.` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Chain vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Bid', back: `The highest price a buyer is currently willing to pay. You sell here if you sell at market.` },
          { front: 'Ask', back: `The lowest price a seller will currently accept. You buy here if you buy at market.` },
          { front: 'Mid', back: `Halfway between bid and ask. A common starting point for a limit order.` },
          { front: 'Volume', back: `How many contracts traded today. Resets to zero each session.` },
          { front: 'Open interest', back: `How many contracts are still open, not yet closed, exercised or expired.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The spread is a toll', html: `<p>Look at the 50 call: bid <strong>$2.40</strong>, ask <strong>$2.60</strong>. If Leo buys at the ask and instantly regrets it and sells at the bid, he loses $0.20 x 100 = <span class="down">$20</span> per contract without the stock moving at all. That gap is the <span class="term" data-def="The difference between the bid and ask prices. A hidden cost every time you trade at market.">bid-ask spread</span>.</p><p>On busy contracts the spread might be a few cents. On thinly traded ones it can be enormous. Imagine a bid of $0.50 and an ask of $1.00. Buying at the ask means the option would have to climb 100% in value just for you to sell it at the bid for what you paid.</p><p>Two habits help:</p><ul><li>Use <strong>limit orders</strong>, often starting near the mid, instead of market orders.</li><li>Prefer contracts with real volume and open interest, where spreads tend to be tighter.</li></ul>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `A market order on an illiquid option is how you pay a dollar for something worth seventy cents. Use limits.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A put shows bid $1.10 and ask $1.30. Dev buys one at the ask and sells it a second later at the bid. How much did the spread cost him in dollars?`, answer: 20, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `$1.30 - $1.10 = $0.20 per share, times 100 shares = $20, before fees.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Volume vs open interest', html: `<p>These two numbers sound alike but measure different things.</p><ul><li><strong>Volume</strong> counts contracts traded today. If Leo buys 5 and later sells them, that adds 10 to volume.</li><li><strong>Open interest</strong> counts contracts that exist right now. It goes up when a new buyer and a new seller open a fresh contract, and down when positions are closed. The figure is updated once a day, so it reflects the previous session.</li></ul><p>A contract can have heavy volume and low open interest (lots of in-and-out day trading) or zero volume today but big open interest (lots of positions just sitting). Both numbers together tell you whether there are enough people around to trade with you at a fair price when you want out.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `If a contract has traded zero times today, its open interest must also be zero.`, answer: false, explain: `Open interest counts contracts already open from earlier days. A contract can be quiet today yet have thousands of open positions.` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Read the ticket twice', html: `<p>Before you send any option order, check five things on the ticket: the underlying, the expiration date, the strike, call or put, and buy or sell. One wrong tap can turn a bullish trade into a bearish one, or a defined-risk purchase into an open-ended sale. Experienced traders still make these slips. The fix is a slow, boring double check.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'When contracts get adjusted', html: `<p>Stock splits, mergers and special dividends can change what an option delivers. When that happens, the OCC adjusts the contract, and it may no longer cover a simple 100 shares. Adjusted contracts often show odd strikes or a changed symbol. If a strike looks strange, like $33.33, check the contract details before trading it.</p>` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `A call is quoted at $3.40. What does one contract cost before fees?`, options: [`$3.40`, `$34`, `$340`, `$3,400`], answer: 2, explain: `$3.40 per share times 100 shares.` },
          { q: `Standard monthly US stock options expire on...`, options: [`The last business day of the month`, `The third Friday of the month`, `The first Monday of the month`, `The 15th of the month`], answer: 1, explain: `Monthlies expire on the third Friday, moved earlier if that day is a market holiday.` },
          { q: `What does open interest measure?`, options: [`Contracts traded today`, `The interest rate used in pricing`, `Contracts currently open and not yet closed`, `The number of buyers only`], answer: 2, explain: `Open interest counts outstanding contracts. Volume counts today's trades.` },
          { q: `A call shows bid $0.90, ask $1.40. What is the safest way to try to buy it?`, options: [`A limit order near the middle, then adjust if needed`, `A market order right away`, `A market order at the open`, `Buy many contracts to average out`], answer: 0, explain: `With a wide spread, a market order pays the full ask. A limit order controls your price.` },
          { q: `In the symbol LMNB 261120P00045000, what is the strike?`, options: [`$261.12`, `$4.50`, `$450`, `$45`], answer: 3, explain: `The last eight digits are the strike times 1,000: 00045000 means $45.000. The P marks it as a put.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
    {
      id: 'options-1-moneyness-value',
      title: 'In, At, or Out of the Money',
      summary: `Learn to tell at a glance whether an option is in, at or out of the money, and split any premium into intrinsic and extrinsic value.`,
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'scale',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Would you use it right now?', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Where an option's strike sits relative to the current stock price: in, at or out of the money.">Moneyness</span> answers a simple question: if you could use this option right this second, would it be worth anything?</p><ul><li><strong>In the money (ITM):</strong> yes. A call is ITM when the stock is <em>above</em> the strike. A put is ITM when the stock is <em>below</em> the strike.</li><li><strong>At the money (ATM):</strong> the stock is at, or very close to, the strike.</li><li><strong>Out of the money (OTM):</strong> no. A call with the stock below the strike, or a put with the stock above it.</li></ul><p>Calls and puts are mirror images, so at any stock price, a call and a put at the same strike are on opposite sides. If the 45 call is in the money, the 45 put is out of the money.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: `Quick test: would you exercise it this second? Yes means in the money. No means out. Close call means at.` } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'moneyness', caption: `With the stock price as the dividing line, calls with strikes below it are in the money, and puts with strikes above it are in the money.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'LMNB at $50', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>Strike</th><th>Call is...</th><th>Put is...</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$40</td><td>ITM (deep)</td><td>OTM (far)</td></tr><tr><td>$45</td><td>ITM</td><td>OTM</td></tr><tr><td>$50</td><td>ATM</td><td>ATM</td></tr><tr><td>$55</td><td>OTM</td><td>ITM</td></tr><tr><td>$60</td><td>OTM (far)</td><td>ITM (deep)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>"Deep" and "far" are informal. A 40 call with the stock at 50 is deep in the money because the stock could drop 20% and it would still be in the money. A 60 call is far out of the money because the stock needs a 20% rally just to reach the strike.</p><p>Moneyness changes every time the stock ticks. Today's OTM option can be ITM tomorrow. Treat the label as a snapshot, not a permanent fact.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `LMNB is at $50. Match each option to its moneyness.`, pairs: [
          { left: '45 call', right: 'In the money' },
          { left: '45 put', right: 'Out of the money' },
          { left: '50 call', right: 'At the money' },
          { left: '58 put', right: 'In the money, by $8' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Intrinsic value: the part you could cash in', html: `<p>An option's premium splits into two pieces. The first is <span class="term" data-def="The amount an option is in the money. What it would be worth if exercised right now. Never below zero.">intrinsic value</span>, the amount it is in the money:</p><ul><li><span class="hl">Call intrinsic = stock - strike</span> (or 0 if negative)</li><li><span class="hl">Put intrinsic = strike - stock</span> (or 0 if negative)</li></ul><p>With LMNB at <strong>$52</strong>:</p><ul><li>50 call: $52 - $50 = <strong>$2</strong> intrinsic</li><li>55 call: below zero, so <strong>$0</strong> intrinsic</li><li>55 put: $55 - $52 = <strong>$3</strong> intrinsic</li></ul><p>Out-of-the-money and at-the-money options have <strong>zero</strong> intrinsic value. Intrinsic value is never negative, because nobody is forced to use a right that would lose money.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Extrinsic value: the price of possibility', html: `<p>Options almost always trade for more than their intrinsic value. The extra is <span class="term" data-def="The part of an option's price above its intrinsic value. Also called time value. It shrinks to zero by expiration.">extrinsic value</span>, also called <strong>time value</strong>:</p><p><span class="hl">Extrinsic = premium - intrinsic</span></p><table><thead><tr><th>LMNB at $52</th><th>Premium</th><th>Intrinsic</th><th>Extrinsic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>50 call</td><td>$3.40</td><td>$2.00</td><td>$1.40</td></tr><tr><td>55 call</td><td>$0.90</td><td>$0</td><td>$0.90</td></tr><tr><td>55 put</td><td>$3.75</td><td>$3.00</td><td>$0.75</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Extrinsic value is what people pay for the <em>chance</em> that the option becomes more valuable before expiration. More time and a jumpier stock mean more possibility, so more extrinsic value. On expiration day, possibility runs out: extrinsic value goes to <strong>zero</strong>, and only intrinsic value is left.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Intrinsic is what the option is worth now. Extrinsic is what people pay for what it might be worth later.` } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'intrinsic-extrinsic', caption: `Each premium stacks two layers: intrinsic value, which only in-the-money options have, and extrinsic value on top, which melts away by expiration.` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A stock trades at $47. Its 45 call is priced at $3.10. How much of that premium is extrinsic value, per share?`, answer: 1.1, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: `Intrinsic is $47 - $45 = $2.00. Extrinsic is $3.10 - $2.00 = $1.10.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Where extrinsic value lives', html: `<p>Extrinsic value is not spread evenly across strikes. It is usually <span class="hl">largest at the money</span> and shrinks as you move away in either direction.</p><ul><li><strong>Deep ITM options</strong> are mostly intrinsic value. A 40 call with LMNB at $50 might cost about $10.30: $10 intrinsic, $0.30 extrinsic. It moves almost dollar for dollar with the stock.</li><li><strong>ATM options</strong> carry the most extrinsic value, because it is a coin flip whether they finish in the money.</li><li><strong>Far OTM options</strong> are 100% extrinsic, but there is not much of it. A 60 call might cost $0.25, all of it hope.</li></ul><p>This matters because extrinsic value is the part that <em>decays</em>. If you buy an OTM option, every cent you paid is extrinsic, and every cent will be gone by expiration unless the stock moves enough to replace it with intrinsic value.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Out-of-the-money option buyers pay entirely for hope. Hope has an expiration date. Literally.` } },
        { type: 'order', prompt: `LMNB is at $50. Order these calls from deepest in the money to furthest out of the money.`, items: [`40 call`, `45 call`, `50 call`, `55 call`, `60 call`], explain: `For calls, lower strikes are further in the money. The 40 call is $10 ITM, while the 60 call needs a $10 rally just to reach its strike.` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A stock trades at $38. What is the intrinsic value of a 45 put, per share?`, answer: 7, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: `Put intrinsic is strike minus stock: $45 - $38 = $7.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why the label matters', html: `<p>Moneyness is not just vocabulary. It shapes almost every decision you will make with options.</p><ul><li><strong>Cost:</strong> ITM options cost more because you are paying for value that already exists. OTM options are cheaper because you are only paying for a chance.</li><li><strong>Odds:</strong> an ITM option is already where it needs to be and just has to stay there. An OTM option needs the stock to travel to the strike and beyond.</li><li><strong>Sensitivity:</strong> deep ITM options move almost like the stock. Far OTM options barely react to small moves, then can jump on big ones.</li><li><strong>Expiration:</strong> whether an option finishes ITM or OTM decides whether it turns into shares or vanishes. You will see in the next lesson that even one cent in the money can trigger automatic exercise.</li></ul><p>Before trading any option, say its moneyness out loud. It is a two-second habit that prevents a lot of confusion.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'A three-step value check', html: `<p>For any option quote: <strong>1.</strong> Is it a call or a put? <strong>2.</strong> Compute intrinsic value: how far in the money is it, or zero? <strong>3.</strong> Subtract intrinsic from the premium to get extrinsic. Now you know exactly how much of the price is real value today and how much is time and hope.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'Maya compares two calls', html: `<p>Maya likes LMNB at $50. She compares the 40 call at $10.30 with the 60 call at $0.25. If LMNB rises $1, the 40 call gains close to $1 because it acts almost like the stock. The 60 call barely budges, because LMNB at $51 is still far from $60. The cheap call only pays off on a big move, and soon. The deep call costs far more but needs much less luck.</p>` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `A stock is at $30. Which option is in the money?`, options: [`A 35 call`, `A 25 put`, `A 25 call`, `A 30 put`], answer: 2, explain: `A call is ITM when the stock is above the strike. $30 is above $25.` },
          { q: `A stock is at $80. A 75 call costs $7.50. What is its extrinsic value?`, options: [`$5.00`, `$2.50`, `$7.50`, `$0`], answer: 1, explain: `Intrinsic is $5. Extrinsic is $7.50 - $5 = $2.50.` },
          { q: `What happens to extrinsic value at expiration?`, options: [`It doubles`, `It turns into intrinsic value`, `It stays the same`, `It goes to zero`], answer: 3, explain: `With no time left, nothing remains to pay for. Only intrinsic value survives.` },
          { q: `Which option usually has the most extrinsic value?`, options: [`At the money`, `Deep in the money`, `Far out of the money`, `All are equal`], answer: 0, explain: `ATM options have the most uncertainty about finishing in the money, so they carry the most time value.` },
          { q: `A stock is at $62. What is the intrinsic value of a 60 put?`, options: [`$2`, `$62`, `$0`, `-$2`], answer: 2, explain: `The put is out of the money because the stock is above the strike. Intrinsic value can never be negative, so it is $0.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
    {
      id: 'options-1-exercise-assignment',
      title: 'Exercise, Assignment & Expiration Day',
      summary: `What actually happens when an option is exercised, why sellers can be assigned early, and how a one-cent move at the close can hand you 100 shares.`,
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'clock',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Three ways an option ends', html: `<p>Every option position ends in one of three ways:</p><ol><li><strong>You close it.</strong> You make the opposite trade before expiration. If you bought, you <em>sell to close</em>. If you sold, you <em>buy to close</em>. Your profit or loss is the difference in premiums.</li><li><strong>It gets exercised.</strong> The holder uses the right, and shares (or cash) actually change hands. On the seller's side, this is called being <span class="term" data-def="Being chosen to fulfill an exercised option: selling shares for a short call or buying shares for a short put.">assigned</span>.</li><li><strong>It expires.</strong> If it is out of the money at expiration, it simply disappears. The buyer loses the premium and the seller keeps it.</li></ol><p>Opening and closing have their own order labels, and your broker will ask for them. Mixing them up can open a new position when you meant to close one.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: `Buy to open, sell to close. Sell to open, buy to close. Say it like a chant until it sticks.` } },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `Match each order label to what it does.`, pairs: [
          { left: 'Buy to open', right: 'Start a new long option position' },
          { left: 'Sell to close', right: 'Exit an option you own' },
          { left: 'Sell to open', right: 'Write a new option and collect premium' },
          { left: 'Buy to close', right: 'Exit an option you previously sold' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'What exercise actually does', html: `<p>When a <strong>call</strong> is exercised, the holder pays the strike price for 100 shares. Leo exercising one LMNB 55 call pays <strong>$5,500</strong> and receives 100 shares. The assigned call seller must deliver those shares, buying them at market if they do not own them.</p><p>When a <strong>put</strong> is exercised, the holder sells 100 shares at the strike. If they do not own the shares, they end up <em>short</em> 100 shares. The assigned put seller must buy those shares and pay the strike price.</p><p>How does a seller get picked? The OCC assigns exercises to brokerage firms at random, and each firm then assigns them to its customers using a fair method, often random selection. Sellers usually find out the next morning, when an assignment notice appears in their account.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'American vs European style', html: `<p>The names have nothing to do with geography. They describe <em>when</em> an option can be exercised.</p><ul><li><strong>American style:</strong> the holder can exercise on any trading day up to expiration. Almost all US options on individual stocks and ETFs are American style.</li><li><strong>European style:</strong> exercise is only possible at expiration. Many index options work this way, including the popular S&amp;P 500 index options known as <strong>SPX</strong>.</li></ul><p>SPX options are also <span class="term" data-def="Settled by paying the in-the-money amount in cash instead of delivering shares.">cash-settled</span>. There are no shares of an index to deliver, so at expiration the in-the-money amount is paid in cash. SPX uses a $100 multiplier, so an SPX call that settles 20 points in the money pays 20 x $100 = $2,000.</p><p>For sellers, European style removes early assignment risk. For American-style options, a seller can be assigned on any day the option is open.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'happy', say: `American style: exercise any day. European style: only at the end. Neither cares which continent you live on.` } },
        { type: 'check', question: `Ms. Ortiz sold an option and wants zero risk of being assigned before expiration day. Which style should she look for?`, options: [`American style`, `European style`, `Any weekly option`, `Any option that is out of the money`], answer: 1, explain: `European-style options can only be exercised at expiration. American-style options, even out-of-the-money ones, can be exercised early, though OTM early exercise is rare.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The one-cent rule', html: `<p>At expiration, you do not have to press an exercise button. The OCC uses a process called <span class="term" data-def="The OCC's procedure that automatically exercises expiring options that are in the money by at least a set threshold, currently $0.01.">exercise by exception</span>: any expiring stock option that is in the money by <span class="hl">$0.01 or more</span> is exercised automatically, unless the holder tells their broker not to.</p><p>Here is how that surprises people. Ms. Ortiz owns one LMNB 55 call. On expiration Friday LMNB closes at <strong>$55.10</strong>. The call is in the money by ten cents, so it is auto-exercised. Over the weekend she owns <strong>100 shares worth about $5,510</strong>, bought for $5,500.</p><p>If Ms. Ortiz does not have $5,500 in her account, her broker may close the option before the close, sell the shares, or issue a margin call. Brokers set their own policies here, often stricter than the OCC's. And if LMNB opens Monday at $50, she is down about $500 on shares she never meant to own.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Leo holds two LMNB 55 calls that finish in the money and are auto-exercised. How much cash does he need to pay for the shares?`, answer: 11000, tolerance: 1, unit: '$', explain: `Each contract buys 100 shares at $55, which is $5,500. Two contracts need $11,000.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why selling usually beats exercising', html: `<p>Two weeks before expiration, LMNB is at $55 and Leo's 50 call is trading at <strong>$6.20</strong>. Intrinsic value is $5. Extrinsic is $1.20.</p><ul><li><strong>Exercise:</strong> he pays $5,000 for shares worth $5,500. Value captured: $500.</li><li><strong>Sell to close:</strong> he receives $6.20 x 100 = <strong>$620</strong>.</li></ul><p>Exercising early throws away the extrinsic value, $120 here, and ties up $5,000 of cash. Selling the option keeps both. That is why most traders close options instead of exercising them, especially for calls on stocks that do not pay dividends.</p><p>Exceptions exist. A deep in-the-money option with almost no extrinsic value left, right before a dividend, can be worth exercising. But the default for most holders is simple: if you want out, sell.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A call with a $40 strike trades at $9.50 while the stock is at $48. How many dollars per contract would Dev give up by exercising instead of selling?`, answer: 150, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `Intrinsic is $8. Extrinsic is $9.50 - $8 = $1.50. Exercising captures only the $8, so he gives up $1.50 x 100 = $150.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Early assignment and dividends', html: `<p>If you sell American-style options, you can be assigned any day. It is uncommon when an option still has plenty of extrinsic value, because the holder would do better selling it. The big exception is <strong>dividends</strong>.</p><p>Only shareholders on record get a dividend. A call holder who wants it must exercise <em>before</em> the <span class="term" data-def="The first day a stock trades without the right to its next dividend.">ex-dividend date</span>. That becomes attractive when the call is in the money and its remaining extrinsic value is smaller than the dividend.</p><p>If you sold that call and get assigned the night before the ex-date, you deliver 100 shares. If you did not own them, you are now <strong>short</strong> 100 shares and you owe the dividend to whoever lent them. Short puts that are deep in the money can be assigned early too, since the holder may prefer the cash now.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Sold an in-the-money call on a dividend stock? Check the ex-dividend date. Assignment loves the night before.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Pin risk', html: `<p>The hardest expiration day is when the stock closes almost exactly at your strike. That is <span class="term" data-def="Uncertainty about assignment when a stock closes very near a short option's strike at expiration.">pin risk</span>.</p><p>Dev sold an LMNB 55 call. Friday's close: <strong>$54.95</strong>. Out of the money, so he relaxes. But holders do not have to decide at 4:00 p.m. The OCC's deadline for submitting exercise instructions is <strong>5:30 p.m. ET</strong> on expiration day, and brokers often set earlier cutoffs. At 4:45 p.m., good news hits and LMNB jumps to $57 in after-hours trading. Some holders exercise their "worthless" calls.</p><p>Monday morning, Dev discovers he is short 100 shares, and LMNB opens at $59. He sold those shares at $55 and they now cost $59 to buy back: a $400 hit, softened only by the small premium he collected. He never saw it coming, and a bigger gap would have hurt far more.</p><p>The standard defense: <span class="hl">close short options that are near the strike before expiration</span>, even if it costs a few cents.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `A call that closes slightly out of the money on expiration day can never be exercised.`, answer: false, explain: `Holders can still submit exercise instructions after the close, before the deadline. After-hours news can make an out-of-the-money call worth exercising.` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Expiration vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Exercise', back: `The holder uses the right: buy shares for a call, sell shares for a put.` },
          { front: 'Assignment', back: `The seller is picked to fulfill an exercise.` },
          { front: 'Exercise by exception', back: `Automatic exercise of options $0.01 or more in the money at expiration.` },
          { front: 'Cash-settled', back: `Pays the in-the-money amount in cash instead of shares, like SPX options.` },
          { front: 'Pin risk', back: `Not knowing if you will be assigned when the stock closes near your strike.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `Most US options on individual stocks are...`, options: [`European style and cash-settled`, `American style and settled in shares`, `European style and settled in shares`, `Only exercisable on Fridays`], answer: 1, explain: `Single-stock options are American style. Exercise delivers actual shares.` },
          { q: `An expiring option is in the money by $0.03 and the holder does nothing. What usually happens?`, options: [`It expires worthless`, `The broker refunds the premium`, `It is automatically exercised`, `It rolls to next month`], answer: 2, explain: `Exercise by exception auto-exercises options $0.01 or more in the money, unless the holder instructs otherwise.` },
          { q: `Why is selling to close usually better than exercising early?`, options: [`Selling captures the extrinsic value too`, `Exercising is illegal before expiration`, `Selling avoids all taxes`, `Exercising always loses money`], answer: 0, explain: `Exercise only captures intrinsic value. The market price of the option also includes the remaining time value.` },
          { q: `When is a short call most at risk of early assignment?`, options: [`When it is far out of the money`, `Right after it is sold`, `On a Monday`, `Just before an ex-dividend date, if it is in the money with little extrinsic value`], answer: 3, explain: `Holders exercise to capture the dividend when it is worth more than the time value they give up.` },
          { q: `What is the best defense against pin risk for an option seller?`, options: [`Wait to see what happens Monday`, `Close short options near the strike before expiration`, `Sell more contracts at the same strike`, `Switch to a market order Monday morning`], answer: 1, explain: `Buying the option back removes the uncertainty about assignment and after-hours moves.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  bossQuestions: [
    { q: `Leo buys two LMNB 60 calls for $1.75 each. LMNB closes at $63 on expiration day. What is his total profit or loss?`, options: [`-$350`, `+$600`, `+$250`, `+$125`], answer: 2, explain: `Each call is worth $3. Profit per share is $3 - $1.75 = $1.25, times 100 shares, times 2 contracts = $250.` },
    { q: `A stock trades at $48. Its 50 put costs $3.30. Which split is correct?`, options: [`Intrinsic $2.00, extrinsic $1.30`, `Intrinsic $3.30, extrinsic $0`, `Intrinsic $0, extrinsic $3.30`, `Intrinsic $1.30, extrinsic $2.00`], answer: 0, explain: `The put is $2 in the money ($50 - $48). The other $1.30 is extrinsic value.` },
    { q: `Ms. Ortiz sells a 40 put for $1.20. At expiration the stock is $35 and she is assigned. Ignoring what she does with the shares afterward, what is her result on the trade at that moment?`, options: [`+$120`, `-$500`, `-$620`, `-$380`], answer: 3, explain: `She pays $40 for shares worth $35, a $5 loss per share, offset by $1.20 collected: -$3.80 x 100 = -$380.` },
    { q: `Dev holds an American-style 50 call on a stock that pays no dividend. The stock is $58 and the call trades at $8.40 with three weeks left. What captures the most value?`, options: [`Exercise now, collecting $800 of value`, `Sell the call for $840`, `Let it auto-exercise and sell the shares Monday`, `Exercise and hold the shares`], answer: 1, explain: `Selling captures the $8 intrinsic value plus $0.40 of extrinsic value. Exercising throws away the extrinsic value and requires $5,000 of cash.` },
    { q: `Which description fits SPX index options?`, options: [`American style, settled in shares, covering 100 shares`, `European style, settled in shares`, `European style, cash-settled, with a $100 multiplier`, `American style, cash-settled, with no multiplier`], answer: 2, explain: `SPX options can only be exercised at expiration and settle in cash, with each index point worth $100.` },
  ],
};
